Compression is the art of saying the same thing with fewer bytes. A file full of the letter a can be described in a handful of bytes, while a file of pure noise cannot be described in fewer bytes than it already uses. Most real files sit somewhere in between: they repeat words, lines, colours and layouts, and a compressor wins by noticing those repetitions and describing them more cheaply than the data itself.
